  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chance for rain showers Thursday evening into \nFriday, though little to no drought improvement anticipated.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA low pressure system centered over the Hudson Bay will move\neastward across Quebec on Thursday, dragging a cold front\nthrough New England Thursday evening into Friday. Highest \nchance of precipitation will be along the Downeast coast \nThursday evening into night. Dry air and lack of strong forcing\nshould keep the north relatively dry Thursday night. There is\nstill a fair amount of uncertainty in how far north the rain\nmakes it Thursday night. Feel pretty confident the coast will\nget some rain, but not sure if it'll get north of Bangor.\nAmounts Downeast are pretty uncertainty, with as little as a\ntenth of an inch and as much as two thirds of an inch depending\non the track of the system. The area remains under D0-D2 \n(abnormally dry to severe drought) conditions, and the \nanticipated rainfall is not expected to improve this. During the\nday Friday, there is a chance of showers in the north with cold\nadvection, but any measurable rain from this is expected to be \nextremely minor and likely less than one quarter inch if any \ndoes fall.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Gusty winds from the northwest during the day \nFriday could lead to isolated power outages.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nCooler air moving in, with a strong pressure gradient, will lead\nto gusty winds over land during the day Friday. Anticipating \nmax gusts 30-40 mph, with about an equal threat areawide. This\nwill be strong enough to lead to isolated power outages like\nwhat we saw back on Monday. The primary threat is during the day\nFriday from mid-morning to sunset.",
